cases.txt holds one case per line; expected.txt is the same file with the answer the JDK of
~/Final/authoring/pebl/bin/ref gave for each case appended in a last tab separated column. It was
produced by handing cases.txt to a java.text / java.math driver through
~/Final/authoring/pebl/bin/jrun, so every expectation in it is the real JDK's output rather than
one someone wrote down. golden_test.go replays it.

The first column is the kind of case:
  D       Double.toString of the double with those bits
  F       Float.toString of the float with those bits
  FMT     new DecimalFormat(pattern, new DecimalFormatSymbols(locale)).format(value)
  DEF     NumberFormat.getInstance(locale).format(value)
  BD      a two operand BigDecimal operation
  BD1     a one operand BigDecimal operation
  P       Double.parseDouble, Long.parseLong, Integer.parseInt, new BigInteger, new BigDecimal
  H       the boxed hashCode
  BADPAT  a DecimalFormat pattern that the JDK rejects
A value is typed by its prefix: L a Long, D a Double by its bits, F a Float by its bits, BD a
BigDecimal, BI a BigInteger, B a Boolean, S a Short, Y a Byte. An answer starting with "!" is the
message of the exception the JDK threw.
